No—the recurring container scan does not appear to delete an already-published image from Artifactory when it becomes inactive. It changes the catalog status to inactive and updates scan/security fields. Any docker image rm commands are runner cleanup, not Artifactory deletion. See [image-recurring-scan.yml (line 657)](/Users/gilbertomendez/RECORE.third-party/.github/workflows/image-recurring-scan.yml:657).
The existing release entry is updated in place: .status and .scanned_date are overwritten during each scan rather than creating a new dated entry. See [image-recurring-scan.yml (line 1223)](/Users/gilbertomendez/RECORE.third-party/.github/workflows/image-recurring-scan.yml:1223).
Recommended approach:
Keep one catalog entry representing the image’s current state.
Emit a separate append-only Splunk event whenever status changes, containing previous_status, new_status, changed_at, and reason.
Do not create another catalog release entry for the same image/version; that would duplicate inventory and distort whitelist totals.

