It is technically possible, but medium-to-high complexity.
An external forwarder could periodically snapshot the catalog JSON files, compare each artifact/version with its previous snapshot, and detect:
New entries
Status transitions such as active → inactive
Changed scanned_date or vulnerability counts
Removals
It would require persistent state/checkpoints, deterministic IDs, support for multiple catalog JSON schemas, duplicate prevention, and handling simultaneous catalog updates.
However, catalog-only forwarding cannot satisfy all telemetry requirements because the JSON files do not contain every workflow event, request timestamp, approval decision, failure reason, or scan-start event. It is viable for current-state and catalog-change reporting, but not as the sole source for complete operational and audit telemetry.
Full snapshots become progressively slower and more expensive as the catalog grows: every run must download, parse, normalize, compare, and checkpoint every entry—even when only one image changed.
You can reduce the impact by storing only a compact hash/state record per artifact_id and processing only catalog files changed since the last commit. However, this effectively becomes a stateful catalog-diff system with checkpointing, merge handling, schema normalization, deduplication, and recovery logic.
So full-catalog snapshots are reasonable as an occasional reconciliation safety net, but they are not a good primary real-time forwarding mechanism. This scalability and operational complexity is one reason write-time telemetry remains the stronger long-term design.
